/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/4746fd4a7c793882f09065278ad9a7350c41f91fcd35677e9ee8768a8ca57b66.png" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534726 true] [17592186305913 1111 #uuid "07c3d370-d067-5363-be4b-84c9dab2acf0" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305914 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/e00df97d8362b857f927d3c572cf09a6e442ff1af639e7cfccaffdded5c4924d.png" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534726 true] [17592186305914 1111 #uuid "a911d95f-c66a-57bf-9bb4-27997272f9f5" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305915 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305916 13194139534726 true] [17592186305915 1089 "q-96457" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305915 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534726 true] [17592186305915 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x-2$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $x=2,$ the slope field is horizontal" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305915 1113 #uuid "26b5af41-aa8e-538e-8148-814f5268dc52" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305915 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,y)}=-2-2=-4<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=1-2=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,y)}=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(2,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/34ab84e5a2b05adad50384cbbb33f48233976aa9e757a40468d5778709801d83.png)" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305917 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305918 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305919 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305920 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305921 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1041 "selection" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305917 13194139534726 true] [17592186305916 1110 #uuid "55462a1c-8e1a-54ba-86d8-3bc4a1241abe" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305917 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305917 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305917 1111 #uuid "488c3d7e-972e-5d22-896b-e1332f1f6c2b" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305918 1034 "I only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305918 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305918 1111 #uuid "c5a8c3e6-d4b4-5eaf-852b-73cfaa32e21d" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305919 1034 "III only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305919 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305919 1111 #uuid "ba089040-a864-53ad-b8f3-b111ca8d6854" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305920 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305920 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305920 1111 #uuid "5e1e9b05-7f6e-5513-b90b-a76506b6c628" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305921 1034 "II only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305921 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305921 1111 #uuid "19156c10-3d31-50d6-9bda-6858c5c4f4d1" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305922 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305923 13194139534726 true] [17592186305922 1089 "q-96460" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305922 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534726 true] [17592186305922 1100 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$ the slope field has a positive slope" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305922 1113 #uuid "2deae67f-b66c-5463-b4cc-a6e8cdf95fcd" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305922 1145 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=(-1)^{3}+(-1)=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=(0)^{2}+0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=(1)^{3}+1=2,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/45427ef9ff00752ad528335ae51143c27fcc01d9c535d2fe380edfbbfdb9b167.png)" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305924 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305925 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305926 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305927 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305928 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1041 "selection" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305924 13194139534726 true] [17592186305923 1110 #uuid "cafc1843-6256-5c96-a316-ceaa9cda3fb6" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305924 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305924 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305924 1111 #uuid "54e1fbd3-d5f5-5631-8c5e-851b88a50197" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305925 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305925 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305925 1111 #uuid "ba5b7e5b-32a5-5635-94a6-36edcd4d91e2" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305926 1034 "I only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305926 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305926 1111 #uuid "3e65d89f-ad53-5995-869e-d9e804392388" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305927 1034 "III only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305927 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305927 1111 #uuid "47f181dd-3a48-5346-8d97-13e0eb37932a" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305928 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305928 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534726 true] [17592186305928 1111 #uuid "3ade0966-1801-597b-a5ec-cf6aaddffe25" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305929 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305930 13194139534726 true] [17592186305929 1089 "q-96627" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305929 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534726 true] [17592186305929 1100 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}+1?$" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305929 1113 #uuid "369f044f-af6a-5ec0-90a8-5d6a9c137079" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305929 1145 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,-2),(1,0),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,0)}=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=(1)^{2}+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/e9cff55c91191b4fa5542d481adf20e29ee55d924da38573ca49ad392fb611bf.png)" 13194139534726 true] [17592186305930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305931 13194139534726 true] [17592186305930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305932 13194139534726 true] [17592186305930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305933 13194139534726 true] [17592186305930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305934 13194139534726 true] [17592186305930 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305935 13194139534726 true] [17592186305930 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 1319413953